## 5. Both leaf train masses are below `0.5304D`

Write the dominant exact orbit as

\[
C\ge U\ge V.
\]

Assume

\[
U\ge U_0:=0.5304.
\]

Use

\[
W<1.912711,
\qquad
E_{dom}>0.90689962.
\]

Set

\[
C_{low}=1.912711-2(0.5304)=0.851911.
\]

For `C<=C_low`, generic center capacity is increasing and already gives a deficit above `0.003`. For `C>=1.0443`, generic center capacity is decreasing and lies more than `1e-5` below `E_dom`.

Thus only

\[
0.851911<C<1.0443
\]

remains. The worst strong center/leaf excess ratio on this interval is

\[
<0.79186<\sqrt{20/29},
\]

so the physical `1/10` pair branch applies to both leaves. The hostile-audited center/leaf correlation gives the one-variable upper capacity

\[
\begin{aligned}
G(C)=&\ C(W_0-C)\\
&-{1\over10}\left(1-{V\over C}\right)(C+U_0-K)^2\\
&-{1\over10}\left(1-{U_0\over C}\right)(C+V-K)^2,
\end{aligned}
\]

where `W_0=1.912711`, `V=W_0-C-U_0`, and `K=2/sqrt(3)`.

The exact derivative calculation gives `G''<-2`. At

\[
c_0=0.931414,
\]

the tangent estimate yields

\[
\max G
\le G(c_0)+{G'(c_0)^2\over4}
<0.906897364.
\]

Hence

\[
E_{dom}-\max G>2.25\times10^{-6},
\]

contradiction. Therefore

\[
\boxed{U,V<0.5304-o(1).}
\tag{E65.13}

---

## 6. Both leaves retain macroscopic good O-O pair weight

For one phase, terminal weights satisfy

\[
a_\tau+b_\tau\le1+\min(a_\tau,b_\tau),
\]

so

\[
z_i\le M_i+P_i+o(1).
\]

Equations (E65.12)--(E65.13) imply

\[
\boxed{P_U,P_V>0.702-0.5304-o(1)=0.1716-o(1).}
\tag{E65.14}

Let `B_bad` be total retained leaf O-O weight whose center degree is at most half of the center family. The hostile-audited center/leaf correlation gives

\[
B_{bad}
\le
{2[C(W-C)-E_{dom}]\over C-U}+o(1).
\]

Using the rounded box above, the inequality `B_bad<0.0367` is equivalent to positivity of a quadratic in `C`. Its exact discriminant satisfies

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{disc}<-2.5\times10^{-4}<0.}
\tag{E65.15}
\]

Thus each leaf retains good O-O weight

\[
\boxed{G_U,G_V>0.1716-0.0367-o(1)=0.1349-o(1).}
\tag{E65.16}

Every good `U` train and good `V` train cross more than half of the same center family, so every cross-leaf pair shares a center neighbor. Triangle-freeness makes that pair noncrossing. Since the two leaf phases differ by `pi/3`, the hostile-audited endpoint theorem gives

\[
e_\tau+e_\sigma\ge2-\sqrt3-o(1).
\]

Summing over the complete weighted product yields

\[
\boxed{
E_U+E_V
>(2-\sqrt3)(0.1349)-o(1).
}
\tag{E65.17}

Using `sqrt(3)<1.7321`, the exact certificate records

\[
E_U+E_V>0.03613971-o(1).
\]

---

## 7. Final contradiction

The selected center-phase occurrence support is disjoint from the selected leaf O-O terminal supports. Hence the center one-well energy and the two-leaf terminal energy add:

\[
\delta
\ge f(z_C)+E_U+E_V-o(1)
>f(0.702)+0.03613971-o(1).
\]

The exact certificate gives

\[
f(0.702)>0.0143259828,
\]

so

\[
\delta>0.0504656928-o(1).
\]

But (E65.7) gives

\[
\delta<0.0504535396.
\]

The exact contradiction margin is

\[
\boxed{>1.21\times10^{-5}.}
\tag{E65.18}

Therefore no full-core Type-II survivor exists with

\[
t\le1.84365.
\]

**Disposition:** proof-only full-core floor raised from `1.84364` to `1.84365`. C-II and Level I remain open.
